 17592186323693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186373533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Particular Solutions of Differential Equations by First Rearranging and Then Separating" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186547177 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186100732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186100894 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "de2e3a7c-d4f0-5b0f-8014-1519fb3a9473" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186101717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Separation of Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186101715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1050 1020.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186101044 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a9b4379c-c4ae-5316-aee1-bbb38b252dff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101715 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101715 #edb/ref 1061 1907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101715 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b9a5578-4e53-599e-9d11-d900a008d201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101715 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186102032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186101717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101716 #edb/ref 1061 1908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50c7a7e0-79f6-53e6-b961-87c7b9f3e5c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101717 #edb/ref 1061 1912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101717 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f16e040-a3f2-5dd6-a8c3-31c41fd50091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186101717 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186100894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102032 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1.$ This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation. \n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(1-2x){y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{{y}^{2}}\\, & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{2}-x-C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0)=\\frac{1}{0-0-C}=1\\,\\Rightarrow \\,C=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of $y' = (1-2x)y^2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by \n\n- separating the variables,\n\n- integrating the equation, and then\n\n- substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102032 #edb/ref 1065 974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102032 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e2c4c33d-a1bd-537e-9daa-1960f765953d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186102032 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining Whether a Point on a Solution Curve Is a Stationary Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157363 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b5586c38-e0ca-5a6d-90ab-71c0fead2cc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186321010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Second Derivative at a Point on a Solution Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157367 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f28fbcc-6d9c-5dc7-89cc-13255db78765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186157990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157368 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1